leges and Schools\nOnline Programs\nLibraries\nContinuing Education\nMilitary Services\nAcademic Calendar\nStudent Services\nRegistrar\nInternational Education\nResearch\nResearch\nResearch and Economic Development\nUndergraduate Research\nPathway to R1\nAdmissions and Aid\nAdmissions and Aid\nApply\nVisit\nNew Student Orientation\nTuition and Payments\nScholarships\nFinancial Aid\nTransfer to South\nOne Stop\nCampus Life\nCampus Life\nHousing\nDining\nOrganizations\nCounseling and Wellness\nStudent Health\nUSA Police\nCampus Recreation and Wellness\nUSA Bookstore\nCommunity Engagement\nSustainability\nAbout\nAbout\nPresident\nEVP Provost\nUniversity Administration\nBoard of Trustees\nFaculty Resources\nUSA History\nStrategic Plan\nBaldwin County Campus\nUSA Health\nMaps\nAlumni\nAlumni\nAlumni Relations\nNAA Board of Directors\nMacQueen Alumni Center\nSports\nSports\nJaguar Athletics\nPurchase Tickets\nAthletics Calendar\nLogins\nStudents\nFaculty & Staff\nPAWS\nUSAonline\nA-Z\nYou have skipped the global top navigation and may now begin browsing the page.\nHome\nColleges\nCollege of Medicine\nDoctoral Program\nHow to Apply\nSkip Left Navigation\nToggle navigationMenu\nCOM Home\nAbout the College\nDean's Office\nLeadership\nAdministrative Offices\nStrategic Plan\nM.D. Program\nCurrent Students\nFuture Students\nOverview\nHow to Apply\nTechnical Standards\nApplication Timeline\nThe Interview Process\nEarly Decision/Early Acceptance\nTuition and Financial Aid\nScholarships and Awards\nThe Curriculum\nAbout USA Health\nMatch Day Results\nOverseas Clinical Experiences\nPrimary Care Pathway\nResearch Opportunities\nBulletin for the M.D. Program\nMBA Healthcare Leadership Concentration\nAccreditation\nAccreditation\nAccreditation Leadership Team\nAccreditation Timeline\nPolicies\nPh.D. Program\nProgram Overview\nCurriculum\nHow to Apply\nCurrent Students\nAlumni Connect\nContact\nOffice of Research Education and Training\nPostdoctoral Fellows\nResidents & Fellows\nOffice of Graduate Medical Education\nGME Programs List\nResidents and Fellows\nProspective Residents and Fellows\nCurrent Residents and Fellows\nClinical Learning Environment\nLife in Mobile\nSalary and Benefits\nVacation and Leaves of Absence\nVirtual Tours of USA Health\nProgram Directors and Coordinators\nFaculty\nAnonymous Reporting\nMBA Healthcare Leadership Concentration\nResident and Fellow Exposition\nFaculty & Staff\nFaculty Affairs and Development\nFaculty Assembly\nStaff Development\nDepartments\nCenters\nResearch\nOverview\nAssociate Dean for Research\nClinical Trials at USA\nCores\nDistinguished Scientists Seminars\nFaculty Research Interests Database\nResearch Seminars and Events\nCOM Research Administration and Development\nOffice of Research Education and Training\nBiostatistics Services\nResearch Equipment Database\nIntramural Grants Program\nDean's Predoctoral Fellowship Award Program\nResearch Opportunities\nAlumni\nMedical Alumni Association\nSupport Your Medical Alumni Association\nMembership Information\nMedical Alumni Awards\nMedical Alumni Reunion\nUpdate Contact Information\nMAA Class Representatives\nMAA Board of Directors\nPast MAA Board Members\nMAA Background\nStay Connected\nSign up for our Newsletter\nDoctoral Program University of South AlabamaMobile, AL 36688 Ph: (251) 460-6153oret@southalabama.edu\nHow to Apply\nThe deadline for applications to the Graduate Program is March 31st each year, though\nearly submission of applications is strongly encouraged. Competitive candidates will\nbe invited to attend one of several on-campus interview days. Students unable to come\nto on-campus interviews will be interviewed via a video call. Matriculation into the\nprogram is limited to the beginning of the Fall term. Information regarding the application\nprocess and the program can be obtained by contacting the Office of Research Education\nand Training by phone (251) 460-6153 or email oret@southalabama.edu.\nApply for Fall 2026\nMinimum Requirements\nA bachelor\u2019s degree from an accredited university or college. For internationals,\nthe bachelor\u2019s\ndegree must have a US equivalent to be considered.\nSatisfactory standing at the last educational institution attended.\nAny international student who has not previously attended at university within the\nUnited\nStates must complete an English proficiency test.\nTwo semesters or three quarters of undergraduate coursework are recommended in each\nof the\nfollowing areas: Physics, General Chemistry, Organic Chemistry, Biology, English Composition,\nand Mathematics (including Calculus and Statistics).\nRequired Application Materials\nApplications will not be considered for review by the Graduate Admissions Committee\nuntil all of the following materials have been received:\nA completed application\nReceipt of transcripts\nPersonal Statement (maximum of 2 pages)\nThree letters of recommendation (requested through the application system)\nFrequently Asked Questions (FAQs)\nDo I need a master\u2019s degree to apply?\nNo, a master\u2019s degree is not required.\nIs research experience required?\nWhile research experience is not required, it is highly recommended.\nDo you offer application fee waivers?\nWe do not offer application fee waivers.\nIs the GRE required for admission?\nBeginning in Fall 2022 admissions, we no longer require GRE test scores for admission.\nDo I need to find a mentor/PI/major professor before joining the program?\nNo, our students complete three rotations within the first year in order to find a\nmentor and laboratory to complete their dissertation research.\nHow do I apply for funding/scholarships?\nAll admitted students receive an annual stipend ($31,000), single-coverage paid health\ninsurance, and full paid tuition from the College of Medicine. Additional financial\naid may be available. For more information, please visit the Office of Financial Aid.\nCan I have a waiver for the English proficiency test score?\nWe do not offer a waiver for the English proficiency score. Any international student\nwho has not previously attended a university/college within the United States is required\nto submit an English proficiency score.\nWhat are the required English proficiency scores for admissions?\nBelow are the scores we recommend for each of the accepted English proficiency tests:\nTOEFL \u2013 100\nIELTS \u2013 7\niTEP \u2013 4.5\nPTE \u2013 68\nDuolingo \u2013 100\nFor TOEFL scores, what is the institution code for the University of South Alabama?\nOur institution code is 1880. There is no department code.\nWhen will I find out the decision on my application?\nThe Admissions Committee generally reviews applications November through April. You\nwill receive an email when a decision has been made. After interviews, offers of admissions\ngenerally go out two to three weeks after the interview date.\nCan I submit unofficial transcripts for the application review?\nYes, we will accept unofficial transcripts for the application review process. However,\nif accepted, you will be required to submit official transcripts.\nMy letter of recommendation writer never received an email from the application system\nto upload their letter. What should I do?\nHave them email the letter with your name directly to oret@southalabama.edu.\nSkip Footer and Return to Top\nSkip Footer and Return to Top\nUniversity of South Alabama\n(251) 460-6101\nMobile, Alabama 36688\nQuick Links\nAlumni\nAthletics\nLibraries\nUSA Health\nMitchell Center\nUSA Bookstore\nResources\nCampus Events\nSustainability\nCampus Safety\nJob Opportunities\nMilitary Services\nOne Stop\nTitle IX\nAccessibility\nPrivacy Statement\n\u00a9 2026 University of South Alabama", "links": [["Skip Main Navigation", "https://www.southalabama.edu/colleges/com/doctoral-program/how-to-apply.html#skippedtopnav"], ["", "https://www.southalabama.edu/"], ["Students", "https://www.southalabama.edu/services/logins/students/"], ["Faculty & Staff", "https://www.southalabam